 -0.01241302490234375, -0.01015472412109375, -0.0246734619140625, 0.02642822265625, 0.007724761962890625, 0.00016772747039794922, 0.0131988525390625, 0.057861328125, 0.03289794921875, -0.025054931640625, -0.0075225830078125, 0.00018990039825439453, 0.035003662109375, -0.00457763671875, -0.0012712478637695312, -0.038055419921875, 0.01294708251953125, -0.0155792236328125, 0.001468658447265625, -0.0234375, -0.0076141357421875, -0.00415802001953125, -0.0258331298828125, 0.0025844573974609375, 0.006072998046875, 0.0165252685546875, -0.002300262451171875, 0.0147857666015625, -0.01087188720703125, -0.0117950439453125, -0.06011962890625, -0.004241943359375, -0.034210205078125, 0.0037097930908203125, -0.002124786376953125, -0.0221099853515625, 0.015106201171875, 0.0128021240234375, 0.0343017578125, 0.0142669677734375, 0.00904083251953125, 0.0218658447265625, 0.01061248779296875, 0.01143646240234375, -0.0109710693359375, -0.00787353515625, -0.00799560546875, 0.0110931396484375, -0.027008056640625, 0.0076446533203125, -0.002750396728515625, 0.003326416015625, 0.0176849365234375, 0.01079559326171875, 0.0048065185546875, -0.0159149169921875, 0.037353515625, -0.00876617431640625, 0.04132080078125, 0.01134490966796875, -0.03466796875, -0.0027332305908203125, -0.00835418701171875, -0.005939483642578125, 0.029327392578125, 0.021759033203125, 0.006778717041015625, 0.0231170654296875, -0.0145721435546875, 0.00734710693359375, -0.01274871826171875, -0.017059326171875, -0.0074920654296875, -7.706880569458008e-05, -0.01348114013671875, -0.003528594970703125, -0.0208587646484375, 0.032257080078125, 0.024078369140625, 0.0184326171875, 0.0070343017578125, -0.031768798828125, -0.0290374755859375, 0.029510498046875, 0.0151519775390625, -0.03936767578125, 0.0516357421875, -0.01580810546875, -0.0423583984375, -0.032257080078125, -0.020965576171875, -0.004543304443359375, 0.044403076171875, 0.0289459228515625, 3.981590270996094e-05, 0.02435302734375, 0.0082855224609375, -0.01177978515625, 0.027374267578125, 0.01513671875, 0.021148681640625, -0.01219940185546875, -0.0305938720703125, 0.0157470703125, 0.016693115234375, -0.0105133056640625, 0.0229644775390625, 0.030059814453125, 0.01629638671875, -0.0194549560546875, 0.016571044921875, 0.0064849853515625, 0.0162200927734375, -0.00797271728515625, -0.01027679443359375, -0.0035991668701171875, -0.0173797607421875, -0.051788330078125, 0.0004570484161376953, -0.01195526123046875, -0.0118255615234375, 0.00865936279296875, -0.0167694091796875, 0.0122222900390625, 0.0009307861328125, 0.00435638427734375, 0.00801849365234375, 0.0242156982421875, 0.043975830078125, -0.021331787109375, 0.01214599609375, 0.03662109375, 0.031768798828125, 0.00039076805114746094, 0.0106353759765625, -0.0252532958984375, -0.0143280029296875, 0.00347900390625, -0.004070281982421875, 0.01183319091796875, 0.01049041748046875, 0.0110626220703125, -0.00638580322265625, -0.013397216796875, -0.0004138946533203125, 0.0104217529296875, -0.0071258544921875, -0.0031490325927734375, -0.00408935546875, -0.01568603515625, -0.0115203857421875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Democracy is a contested concept, and how it is defined has significant implications for both academic research and policymaking. This document argues that the choice between minimalist (also called procedural) and maximalist (also called substantive) definitions of democracy fundamentally shapes not only scholarly understanding but also practical assessments and policy decisions regarding political systems. The unique contribution of this document is to demonstrate, through systematic comparison and analysis, how definitional choices\u2014whether democracy is defined narrowly by its procedures or more broadly to include substantive rights and freedoms\u2014directly affect how regimes are evaluated, compared, and engaged with worldwide.\n\n**Defining Democracy: Minimalist (Procedural) vs. Maximalist (Substantive) Approaches**\n\nA **minimalist (procedural) definition** of democracy focuses on the existence of regular, competitive, and inclusive elections that allow citizens to choose their leaders. Joseph Schumpeter (1942) famously argued that democracy is best understood as an institutional arrangement for arriving at political decisions through a competitive struggle for the people\u2019s vote. In this view, democracy is defined by clear, observable procedures\u2014universal suffrage, secret ballots, and the peaceful transfer of power\u2014rather than by outcomes or broader social context. For example, contemporary Russia holds regular elections, but opposition parties face significant barriers, media is tightly controlled, and electoral outcomes are often predetermined (OSCE 2021; Freedom House 2022). This suggests that even when procedural forms are present, the reality may be undemocratic, highlighting the limitations of a purely procedural approach.\n\nIn contrast, a **maximalist (substantive) definition** incorporates not only electoral procedures but also a broader set of requirements, such as the protection of civil liberties, human rights, and the rule of law. Robert Dahl (1971) introduced the concept of \"polyarchy\" to describe real-world approximations of democracy, emphasizing both contestation (competition among elites) and participation (inclusiveness of the electorate). Polyarchy requires not just free and fair elections but also freedoms of speech, assembly, and association, as well as equal legal protection. Maximalist criteria thus include both democratic practices (e.g., competitive elections, independent judiciary) and democratic values (e.g., political equality, protection of minority rights). Sweden exemplifies a maximalist democracy: it combines robust electoral competition with strong protections for civil liberties, a free press, and inclusive social policies (Reporters Without Borders 2022; Freedom House 2022).\n\n**Systematic Comparison: Case Studies**\n\nA more systematic comparison of cases illustrates the implications of these definitions. In each case, we specify which features correspond to procedural (minimalist) and substantive (maximalist) criteria:\n\n- **Russia**: Holds regular elections (procedural/minimalist criterion), but severe restrictions on opposition, media, and civil society (substantive/maximalist criteria) undermine both procedural integrity and substantive democracy (OSCE 2021; Freedom House 2022). For example, the 2021 Duma elections were criticized for lack of genuine competition and media freedom (OSCE/ODIHR, 2021 Final Report).\n- **Turkey**: Regular elections are held (procedural), but increasing restrictions on media, civil society, and opposition parties (substantive) erode substantive rights (Human Rights Watch 2022; Freedom House 2022). Thus, Turkey may be classified as democratic under minimalist definitions but not under maximalist ones. Freedom House (2022) notes, \"The government\u2019s control over the judiciary and media has undermined checks and balances.\"\n- **India**: Known for vibrant elections and peaceful transfers of power (procedural), yet ongoing concerns about civil liberties and minority rights\u2014such as restrictions on NGOs and communal violence (substantive)\u2014challenge its status as a substantive democracy (Amnesty International 2021; Human Rights Watch 2022). For instance, Human Rights Watch (2022) documents \"increasing harassment of journalists and activists.\"\n- **Sweden**: Meets both procedural and substantive criteria, with competitive elections (procedural), strong civil liberties, and high rankings in global indices for press freedom and civil rights (substantive) (Reporters Without Borders 2022; Freedom House 2022).\n\nThese examples demonstrate how the choice of definition can lead to divergent assessments of the same country and reveal gray areas where regimes may meet some but not all criteria. This underscores the central thesis: definitional choices shape both scholarly and policy judgments.\n\n**Critiques and Counterarguments**\n\nMinimalist definitions are often criticized, notably by Larry Diamond and advocates of liberal democracy, for ignoring substantive elements such as the protection of rights and the inclusiveness of participation. They argue that focusing solely on procedures risks legitimizing regimes that are democratic in form but not in substance. For example, labeling Russia or Turkey as democracies based solely on elections overlooks significant violations of rights and freedoms. However, proponents of minimalism, such as Adam Przeworski, counter that including a wide range of civil liberties and social rights makes the concept of democracy too broad and difficult to operationalize. Przeworski (2000) argues that democracy should be defined by clear, observable procedures to allow for objective comparison and to avoid subjective judgments about rights, which can vary across contexts.\n\nCritics of maximalism note that the measurement of rights can be subjective and context-dependent, complicating comparative research and leading to disagreements over classification. For example, the classification of \"hybrid regimes\" or \"illiberal democracies\" (Levitsky & Way 2010) highlights the challenge of regimes that combine procedural elections with substantive deficits. Measurement challenges are evident in the varying scores assigned by Freedom House and the Economist Intelligence Unit to countries like Hungary, reflecting different emphases on procedural versus substantive criteria. There is also debate about whether some rights (e.g., social or economic rights) should be considered essential to democracy or are better seen as outcomes of democratic governance.\n\n**Policy Implications and Real-World Consequences**\n\nThe distinction between minimalist and maximalist definitions is not merely academic; it has concrete policy implications. International organizations such as Freedom House and the Economist Intelligence Unit use different criteria to rate countries, which can influence foreign aid decisions, diplomatic recognition, and the design of democracy promotion programs. For example, after the Arab Spring, some transitional governments received international support based on their adoption of electoral procedures (procedural/minimalist), even as civil liberties remained restricted (substantive/maximalist) (Freedom House 2012). Similarly, the classification of Hungary as a \"flawed democracy\" by the Economist Intelligence Unit, due to declining press freedom and judicial independence, has affected EU funding and diplomatic relations (Economist Intelligence Unit 2022). According to the EIU (2022), Hungary\u2019s score dropped from 6.72 in 2015 to 6.50 in 2022, reflecting substantive declines. These cases show how definitional choices can lead to policy missteps, such as supporting regimes that meet procedural but not substantive standards, or withholding support from countries making progress on rights but lacking perfect electoral procedures. Policymakers and researchers should therefore be explicit about the definitions they employ and consider the potential consequences of their choices. Democracy promotion initiatives that focus only on electoral assistance may overlook deeper issues of rights and freedoms, while broader programs may face challenges in measurement and implementation.\n\n**Conclusion and Recommendations**\n\nIn conclusion, the debate over how to define democracy is central to both scholarship and policy. Definitional clarity is essential for meaningful comparative analysis, effective democracy promotion, and informed judgments about political systems. Scholars and policymakers should be transparent about their criteria, recognize the trade-offs involved in different approaches, and be attentive to the gray areas between procedural and substantive dimensions. Actionable recommendations include: (1) explicitly stating and justifying definitional choices in research and policy documents, for example by including a dedicated section on definitions and criteria; (2) using multiple indicators\u2014such as combining election quality indices with civil liberties and rule of law measures\u2014to capture both procedural and substantive aspects of democracy; and (3) ensuring transparency by publishing the sources, coding rules, and data underlying democracy assessments, and by regularly reviewing and updating these criteria in light of new evidence. Ultimately, understanding the diversity of democratic practices and values worldwide requires careful attention to both procedures and rights, supported by precise definitions and systematic analysis.\n\n**References**\n\n- Schumpeter, J. A. (1942). *Capitalism, Socialism and Democracy*. New York: Harper & Brothers.\n- Dahl, R. A. (1971). *Polyarchy: Participation and Opposition*. New Haven: Yale University Press.\n- Przeworski, A., Alvarez, M. E., Cheibub, J. A., & Limongi, F. (2000). *Democracy and Development: Political Institutions and Well-Being in the World, 1950\u20131990*. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press.\n- Levitsky, S., & Way, L. A. (2010). *Competitive Authoritarianism: Hybrid Regimes after the Cold War*. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press.\n- Freedom House. (2022). *Freedom in the World 2022: The Global Expansion of Authoritarian Rule*. https://freedomhouse.org/report/freedom-world/2022\n- Freedom House. (2012). *Freedom in the World 2012: The Arab Uprisings and Their Global Repercussions*. https://freedomhouse.org/report/freedom-world/2012\n- OSCE/ODIHR. (2021). *Russian Federation, State Duma Elections, 17\u201319 September 2021: ODIHR Limited Election Observation Mission Final Report*. https://www.osce.org/odihr/elections/russia/505526\n- Human Rights Watch. (2022). *World Report 2022: Events of 2021*. https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2022\n- Amnesty International. (2021). *India 2021"}}